WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.141
Between the kitchen and the dining room of an old house there's a hatch: a small window in the wall, with a shelf.

2
00:00:09.141 --> 00:00:14.169
The cook puts a plate on the shelf, and on the other side a helper takes it to the table.

3
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.068
The shelf holds two plates.

4
00:00:16.068 --> 00:00:17.982
When it's full, the cook waits.

5
00:00:17.982 --> 00:00:20.082
When it's empty, the helper waits.

6
00:00:20.082 --> 00:00:23.664
And unlike a ring of the bell, a plate is eaten only once.

7
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:27.836
Last lesson, the board and the bell sent every value to everyone.

8
00:00:27.836 --> 00:00:30.829
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

9
00:00:30.829 --> 00:00:34.005
How do you hand each plate to exactly one coroutine?

10
00:00:34.005 --> 00:00:38.891
And when the cook is done, how does the helper know that nothing more is coming?

11
00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:44.162
Kotlin's hatch is a Channel.

12
00:00:44.162 --> 00:00:49.547
The cook calls send to put a plate in, and the helper calls receive to take one out.

13
00:00:49.547 --> 00:00:51.983
Each element is received exactly once.

14
00:00:51.983 --> 00:00:56.919
The shelf is the capacity: how many plates can wait before send has to pause.

15
00:00:56.919 --> 00:01:02.368
And waiting here means suspending: a helper at an empty hatch holds no thread at all.

16
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.040
Let's time it.

17
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:10.887
The cook puts five plates through as fast as she can, and the helper needs 100 milliseconds per plate.

18
00:01:10.887 --> 00:01:12.229
The shelf holds two.

19
00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:14.083
When does each send return?

20
00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:26.104
The first three return at once: plate 1 goes straight into the waiting helper's hands, and plates 2 and 3 sit on the shelf.

21
00:01:26.104 --> 00:01:29.631
Plate 4 finds the shelf full, so the cook waits.

22
00:01:29.631 --> 00:01:34.260
Then one send returns every 100 milliseconds: at 101, then 201.

23
00:01:34.733 --> 00:01:36.809
The slow helper set the cook's pace.

24
00:01:36.809 --> 00:01:40.443
That's called backpressure, and it keeps plates from piling up.

25
00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.916
The shelf size changes everything.

26
00:01:42.916 --> 00:01:50.986
With no shelf at all, the default, called rendezvous, the cook hands each plate over directly, one every 100 milliseconds.

27
00:01:50.986 --> 00:01:53.037
UNLIMITED never makes her wait.

28
00:01:53.037 --> 00:01:59.851
And CONFLATED keeps one spot, where each new plate replaces the old one: the helper got plates 1 and 5.

29
00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:07.725
UNLIMITED sounds generous, until the dining room is full of plates and the program runs out of memory, some hours later.

30
00:02:07.725 --> 00:02:10.277
So be suspicious of a shelf with no end.

31
00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.920
Now the trap: the last plate.

32
00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.371
The helper loops: for each dish in the hatch, serve it.

33
00:02:16.371 --> 00:02:17.877
When does that loop end?

34
00:02:17.877 --> 00:02:20.512
Only when the channel is closed and empty.

35
00:02:20.512 --> 00:02:23.336
Delete the close, and the program never ends.

36
00:02:23.567 --> 00:02:29.500
The helper stands at the hatch all night with an empty tray, waiting for a plate that will never come.

37
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.979
Here the picture breaks: a real helper would see the cook take off her apron.

38
00:02:33.979 --> 00:02:37.876
A coroutine can't tell later from never, until someone calls close.

39
00:02:37.876 --> 00:02:40.959
Or let produce close it for you, when its block ends.

40
00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:48.366
One helper carries one plate at a time, so twenty dishes of about 100 milliseconds take two seconds.

41
00:02:48.366 --> 00:02:52.788
The fix in a real kitchen is plain: more helpers at the same hatch.

42
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:01.903
That's fan-out: several coroutines receive from one channel, and each plate still goes to exactly one of them.

43
00:03:01.903 --> 00:03:06.678
With four helpers, the twenty dishes took 614 milliseconds.

44
00:03:06.678 --> 00:03:09.916
One helper took 2,181, and ten took 324.

45
00:03:10.133 --> 00:03:16.678
And fan-in is the way back: every helper sends finished plates into a second channel, read at one table.

46
00:03:16.678 --> 00:03:23.096
Close it after the whole team is done, from a coroutine of its own, or that table waits all night too.

47
00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.808
Why not start an async for every dish?

48
00:03:25.808 --> 00:03:34.122
Because ordering 5,000 loaves that way means 5,000 phone calls in the same second, and the baker takes the phone off the hook.

49
00:03:34.122 --> 00:03:36.696
A team of N helpers caps the work at N.

50
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:38.453
Last, a race.

51
00:03:38.453 --> 00:03:41.311
The cook phones two bakeries for bread.

52
00:03:41.311 --> 00:03:46.735
The corner bakery takes 300 milliseconds to answer, the market bakery 120.

53
00:03:46.735 --> 00:03:50.619
She takes the first answer and hangs up on the other.

54
00:03:50.619 --> 00:03:53.917
If neither answers in 500, she bakes her own.

55
00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:56.231
Which answer does select return?

56
00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:06.901
The market bakery's: plenty of sourdough, after 213 milliseconds on a cold, busy machine, not quite 120.

57
00:04:06.901 --> 00:04:12.528
select waits for the first clause that's ready, runs it, and drops the rest.

58
00:04:12.528 --> 00:04:18.377
With a budget of 100 milliseconds, the timer wins, and she bakes her own bread.

59
00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:19.281
Two rules.

60
00:04:19.281 --> 00:04:23.366
If two clauses are ready at once, the first one listed wins.

61
00:04:23.366 --> 00:04:27.111
And hanging up is your job: cancel the losers yourself.

62
00:04:27.933 --> 00:04:31.759
Here's the hatch in Kotlin, with Java's BlockingQueue below.

63
00:04:31.759 --> 00:04:35.648
A queue can't be closed, so Java sends an end marker instead.

64
00:04:37.433 --> 00:04:40.876
So, how do you hand each plate to exactly one coroutine?

65
00:04:40.876 --> 00:04:42.659
Through a channel, the hatch.

66
00:04:42.659 --> 00:04:52.557
The shelf sets the pace, a team of helpers caps the work, select takes whichever answer comes first, and close is the cook's word that the last plate is through.

67
00:04:52.767 --> 00:04:53.629
Quick check.

68
00:04:53.629 --> 00:04:59.807
A hatch with no shelf, a fast cook, and a helper who needs 100 milliseconds per plate.

69
00:04:59.807 --> 00:05:01.747
How often does send return?

70
00:05:04.733 --> 00:05:07.013
About once every 100 milliseconds.

71
00:05:07.013 --> 00:05:10.635
With no shelf, each send waits for the helper's hands.
